{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.final-object.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.141c0f3fd90dc7b4.final-object","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"An initial or final universal arrow property presupposes an ambient category. Stacks uses a set-sized object collection for its underlying category; this link is not a blanket identification with arbitrary large categories.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002B","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.initial-object.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.141c0f3fd90dc7b4.initial-object","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"The initial-object universal property uses objects, morphisms and composition of an ambient category; Stacks small-category axioms supply this prerequisite within the source's set-size convention.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002B","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.representable-covariant-functor.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.0e1768811a8dab34.representable-covariant-functor","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"functor F:C","editorial_reason":"A representable covariant set-valued functor acts on a category with Hom sets. The screened Stacks small-category is a suitable ambient source convention, but does not supply a general functor definition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0AHM","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.monoidal-category.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.2ec41283717d02b0.monoidal-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"A monoidal category equips an existing category with tensor, unit and coherence data; these are additional properties, so an ordinary category is definitionally prior in the Stacks small-object setting.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FFJ","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.discrete-category.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.3653fa0ad844d187.discrete-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"The Stacks definition of a discrete category restricts the ambient category's morphisms to identities. The general small-category structure logically precedes this restriction.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0042","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.filtered-category.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.9d27e470d02eae69.filtered-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category I","editorial_reason":"Filteredness imposes object-directedness and coequalization of parallel arrows on an existing category, making the general category axioms prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04AX","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.connected-category.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.daec56e6a2a51cae.connected-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category I","editorial_reason":"Connectedness of a category is a zigzag condition on arrows connecting its objects. It depends on a category, and is different from topological connectedness.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04AQ","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.opposite-category.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.fef4e69e96c25bfa.opposite-category","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"The opposite-category construction starts with the objects and arrows of an existing category and reverses arrows and composition; the ambient category is the prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/001L","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.categorical-epimorphism.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.eed0c34725956bb5.categorical-epimorphism","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"An epimorphism requires right cancellation of composable arrows in a category, not merely set-theoretic surjectivity; the small-category concept supplies the ambient arrow structure.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003A","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.categorical-monomorphism.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.eed0c34725956bb5.categorical-monomorphism","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"category C","editorial_reason":"Categorical monomorphisms are defined by left cancellation of composable arrows; they depend on ordinary category structure rather than a generic set-injection definition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/003A","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.category-colimit.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.8f7683667fac9d28.category-colimit","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"diagram F:I→C","editorial_reason":"The colimit universal property requires categories of diagram indices and target objects, with compatible arrows. The small-category structure supplies one prerequisite, while functor and cocone remain separate gaps.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002D","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.category-limit.to.small-category","from_formulation":"form.8f7683667fac9d28.category-limit","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.small-category","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"diagram F:I→C","editorial_reason":"The limit universal property requires an ambient category, diagram arrows and compatible cones. The Stacks small-category axioms supply the ambient prerequisite without supplying a separate cone definition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/002D","target_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0013","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.connected-topological-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.ab260940ea9c523d.connected-topological-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"Connectedness is an additional axiom on decompositions of a nonempty topological space into open subsets. Stacks explicitly excludes the empty space while the underlying topology allows it.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004R","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.extremally-disconnected-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.b040ab223731507a.extremally-disconnected-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"Extremal disconnectedness constrains closures of open subsets in a topological space; it presupposes the general open-set topology, not a specific spectral topology.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YH","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.jacobson-topological-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.a757d94fc61ac37f.jacobson-topological-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"Jacobson-space properties refer to closed points dense in closed subsets of an ambient topology, for which the general topological-space axioms are prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/005T","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.locally-closed-partition.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.b1c99b8dae7a6bde.locally-closed-partition","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"Partitions into locally closed sets presuppose an ambient topological space and its open and closed subsets. The linked general topology does not itself define a partition.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09XY","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.locally-finite-collection.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.b1c99b8dae7a6bde.locally-finite-collection","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"open neighbourhood","editorial_reason":"Local finiteness quantifies over open neighbourhoods of each point in X. This requires a topology but not any additional compactness hypothesis.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09XY","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.interior-subset-topological.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.c15e2b26c207d87e.interior-subset-topological","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"The interior of A is defined as the union of open subsets of the ambient topological space lying in A. The general open-set axioms are the appropriate prerequisite.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03HM","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.irreducible-topological-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.e0dd0b0cffcfff0b.irreducible-topological-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"Irreducibility states that a nonempty topological space cannot be the union of two proper closed subsets, presupposing only the ambient topology and not a scheme.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/004U","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.hausdorff-space.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.e62923cd8ef99cd1.hausdorff-space","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"The Hausdorff separation axiom presupposes open neighbourhoods in a topological space; it is an additional condition on that space, not part of basic topology axioms.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZD","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.constructible-subset-stacks.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.eea0cd4a31af555c.constructible-subset-stacks","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topological space","editorial_reason":"This Stacks constructibility definition involves retrocompact open subsets of a topological space. The ambient topology is the prerequisite, while retrocompactness remains a separate concept.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04ZC","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.submersive-continuous-map.to.topological-space","from_formulation":"form.83a528850cdf6a59.submersive-continuous-map","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.topological-space","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"topology","editorial_reason":"Submersiveness compares the codomain topology with the inverse-image topology of a surjective continuous map, so it presupposes domain and codomain topological-space structures.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0405","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ATopological_Space","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.zerodivisor-of-ring.to.associative-ring-identity-optional","from_formulation":"form.559efcb6f15ddc96.zerodivisor-of-ring","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.associative-ring-identity-optional","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"commutative ring","editorial_reason":"A zero-divisor is defined using multiplication in a commutative ring, whose underlying associative ring structure is a valid broad prerequisite. The commutative-unital context is not erased by linking.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02LV","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARing_%28Abstract_Algebra%29","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
{"id":"rel.wave2.batch03.artinian-commutative-ring.to.two-sided-ideal","from_formulation":"form.9442fc10b22be057.artinian-commutative-ring","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.two-sided-ideal","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","source_phrase":"ideals","editorial_reason":"Artinianity is the descending-chain condition on ideals of a commutative ring, and the ordinary two-sided ideal concept supplies the necessary ideal absorption structure.","source_locator":"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00J4","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AIdeal_of_Ring","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_stacks_mathematical_review_2026_10_19","human_verified":false}
